{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"escape velocity escape velocity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md","fileName":"Escape Velocity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Escape Velocity","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-16 21:32\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Orbital Mechanics and Unmanned Flight]] · [[Gravitation and Relativity]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Orbital Mechanics and Unmanned Flight","Gravitation and Relativity","flightscience.pdf","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"escape velocity escape velocity","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md","fileName":"Escape Velocity.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Escape Velocity","heading":"Escape Velocity","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Escape velocity is the minimum ideal launch speed that lets an object reach indefinitely large distance with no remaining speed under gravity alone. It follows by equating initial kinetic energy with the gravitational potential-energy change and decreases as launch distance from the planet's center increases. For Earth it is about 11.2 kilometers per second near the surface when atmospheric resistance and rotation are neglected.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Orbital Mechanics and Unmanned Flight","Gravitation and Relativity","flightscience.pdf","foundationsofphysics.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Escape Velocity.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"escape velocity e